Yes. This is a coherent extension of the finite-state-machine idea, and it gives you a mathematical foundation for your earlier **Mathematical Futurology** concept.

The key move is:

> **A finite-state machine has a state space and transition rule. An Infinite State Machine generalizes this by allowing the state space, transition structure, or both to be infinite. Time can then be modeled as the trajectory of a system through an infinite state space.**

## 1. Start with the ordinary FSM

A deterministic finite-state machine can be written

$$
\mathcal F=(S,\Sigma,\delta,s_0),
$$

where:

* \(S\) = finite set of states
* \(\Sigma\) = set of inputs
* \(\delta:S\times\Sigma\to S\) = transition function
* \(s_0\) = initial state.

For a sequence of inputs

$$
x_0,x_1,x_2,\ldots
$$

we obtain

$$
s_{t+1}=\delta(s_t,x_t).
$$

So an FSM gives us something very important:

$$
\boxed{\text{state at }t \longrightarrow \text{state at }t+1}
$$

This is already a primitive theory of time.

But ordinary FSMs usually treat \(t\) as an external index.

Your theory makes **time itself the state-transition structure**.

---

# 2. The Infinite State Machine

Define an Infinite State Machine as

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal I=(S,\Sigma,\delta,s_0)
}
$$

where

$$
|S|=\infty.
$$

The simplest example is

$$
S=\mathbb N
$$

with

$$
\delta(n)=n+1.
$$

Then:

$$
0\rightarrow1\rightarrow2\rightarrow3\rightarrow\cdots
$$

There is no final state.

The sequence itself is therefore an infinite temporal trajectory.

We can write

$$
s(t)=\delta^t(s_0).
$$

This gives us a first definition:

> **Time is the ordered sequence of state transitions generated by a dynamical system.**

---

# 3. But Infinity lets us go much further

There are actually several different kinds of infinity.

### Type I — Infinite duration

$$
t\in\mathbb N
$$

The system continues indefinitely.

### Type II — Infinitely many states

$$
|S|=\infty.
$$

There may be infinitely many possible configurations.

### Type III — Continuous time

$$
t\in\mathbb R.
$$

There are infinitely many temporal positions between any two positions.

### Type IV — Infinite-dimensional state

The state itself can be something like

$$
S=\mathbb R^\infty.
$$

This is much more powerful.

Your theory should therefore **not define an Infinite State Machine merely as an FSM with infinitely many states**.

Instead:

$$
\boxed{
\text{FSM}
\subset
\text{Infinite-State Machine}
\subset
\text{General Dynamical System}
}
$$

---

# 4. Time as a state machine

Now make the conceptual reversal.

Instead of saying:

> A system evolves **through time**,

say:

> **Time is the evolution of a system through states.**

Let

$$
\mathcal U=(S,T,\Phi)
$$

be a temporal state system, where:

* \(S\) is the universe of possible states;
* \(T\) is the temporal domain;
* \(\Phi\) is the evolution operator.

For discrete time:

$$
T=\mathbb Z
$$

and

$$
\Phi:S\times\mathbb Z\rightarrow S.
$$

The state at time \(t\) is

$$
X_t=\Phi(X_0,t).
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Time}=
\{X_t:t\in T\}
}
$$

rather than time being an independent substance.

This is the beginning of a **state-based ontology of time**.

---

# 5. The FuturLang interpretation

This is where FuturLang becomes genuinely interesting.

Your language already distinguishes between:

* an object/state,
* an observation,
* and a proposition requiring proof.

We can add a fourth primitive:

$$
\boxed{\tau}
$$

for temporal transition.

Then we can describe reality as:

$$
X_t
\xrightarrow{\tau}
X_{t+1}.
$$

A future is therefore not necessarily a single object.

It is a set of reachable states.

Define the transition relation

$$
X\rightarrow Y.
$$

Then the future of \(X\) is

$$
\operatorname{Future}(X)
=
\{Y:X\rightarrow^*Y\}.
$$

Here \(\rightarrow^*\) means "reachable through zero or more transitions."

That gives Mathematical Futurology a rigorous object:

$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{Future}(X)
}
$$

is the reachable-state space of \(X\).

---

# 6. The future becomes a graph

This produces a beautiful connection to the graph theory in Urschel's thesis.

Instead of representing time as a line:

$$
t_0\rightarrow t_1\rightarrow t_2\rightarrow t_3,
$$

represent possible time evolution as a directed graph:

$$
X_0
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
X_1\\
X_2\\
X_3
\end{cases}
$$

because a state may have multiple possible successors.

The resulting structure is a **temporal state graph**.

Define

$$
G_T=(S,E_T)
$$

where

$$
(x,y)\in E_T
$$

iff \(y\) is reachable from \(x\) in one temporal transition.

Now:

$$
\boxed{
\text{time evolution}=\text{path through a state graph}.
}
$$

A deterministic universe has one successor:

$$
|\operatorname{Succ}(X)|=1.
$$

A nondeterministic universe can have:

$$
|\operatorname{Succ}(X)|>1.
$$

This gives you a mathematical way to discuss **possible futures without claiming that all possibilities actually occur**.

---

# 7. Infinity changes the meaning of "future"

For a finite machine, you can potentially enumerate all states.

For an infinite machine:

$$
S=\{s_0,s_1,s_2,\ldots\}
$$

there may be no final state.

But there is another possibility:

$$
S=\mathbb R.
$$

Then the state space is uncountably infinite.

This distinction matters.

We can define:

### Discrete Infinite Time

$$
T=\mathbb Z.
$$

### Continuous Time

$$
T=\mathbb R.
$$

### Infinite-dimensional time/state

$$
T\subseteq\mathcal F
$$

for some function space \(\mathcal F\).

Your theory can therefore ask:

> **What mathematical structure does time possess?**

rather than assuming beforehand that it is simply \(\mathbb R\).

---

# 8. The Infinite State Machine of the Universe

Now we can formulate the strongest version.

Let

$$
\mathcal U=(S,\rightarrow,X_0)
$$

be the **Universal Infinite State Machine**, where:

* \(S\) = set of physically/mathematically possible states;
* \(X_0\) = initial state;
* \(\rightarrow\) = lawful state transition relation.

A history is a sequence

$$
H=(X_0,X_1,X_2,\ldots)
$$

such that

$$
X_i\rightarrow X_{i+1}.
$$

Then:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Time is the ordering of states within a history.}
}
$$

This gives a precise version of the idea that the universe is "computing."

The universe need not literally be a computer.

Rather, its evolution has the mathematical structure of a transition system.

---

# 9. Now introduce infinity formally

Here is where your theory becomes more than an ordinary FSM.

Suppose

$$
X_0\rightarrow X_1\rightarrow X_2\rightarrow\cdots
$$

never terminates.

Then we have an infinite trajectory

$$
H:\mathbb N\rightarrow S.
$$

But we can also consider its limit behavior.

If

$$
\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}X_t=X_\infty,
$$

then \(X_\infty\) is a limiting state.

Importantly:

$$
X_\infty
$$

doesn't necessarily have to be reached at a finite time.

So we distinguish:

$$
\boxed{\text{reachable}}
$$

from

$$
\boxed{\text{approachable}}
$$

from

$$
\boxed{\text{possible}}
$$

from

$$
\boxed{\text{actual}}.
$$

That distinction could become one of the central axioms of Futurlang.

---

# 10. The "future" is an information problem

This connects directly to your earlier reconstruction idea.

Suppose we observe

$$
O(X_t).
$$

We don't necessarily know \(X_t\).

Therefore we have an information state

$$
I_t.
$$

The future isn't necessarily

$$
X_{t+1}.
$$

From our perspective it may be the set

$$
\mathcal F(I_t)
=
\{X_{t+1}:X_t\text{ is compatible with }I_t\}.
$$

So Mathematical Futurology becomes:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Given present information, characterize reachable future states.}
}
$$

That is a rigorous mathematical formulation of **prediction**.

---

# 11. The central theorem-like principle

You could formulate the foundational principle of the theory as:

> **Infinite-State Temporal Principle**
>
> A temporal system can be represented as an ordered transition system
>
> $$
> \mathcal T=(S,\rightarrow)
> $$
>
> whose histories are paths through \(S\). Time is represented by the ordering of states along a history, while the future is represented by the set of states reachable from the current state.

Then define:

$$
\operatorname{Past}(X)
=
\{Y:Y\rightarrow^*X\}
$$

and

$$
\operatorname{Future}(X)
=
\{Y:X\rightarrow^*Y\}.
$$

This gives you a beautiful symmetry:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Past}(X)
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
X
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{Future}(X)
}
$$

---

# 12. Where "Infinity" becomes philosophically powerful

The ordinary FSM has a beginning:

$$
s_0.
$$

Your infinite temporal machine can remove that assumption.

Consider

$$
\ldots\rightarrow X_{-2}\rightarrow X_{-1}
\rightarrow X_0
\rightarrow X_1\rightarrow X_2\rightarrow\ldots
$$

Now there is neither an initial nor terminal state.

Time becomes a **bi-infinite trajectory**:

$$
H:\mathbb Z\rightarrow S.
$$

Or we could have

$$
H:\mathbb R\rightarrow S.
$$

This gives three major models:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Finite time:}&\quad [0,T]\\
\text{One-sided infinite time:}&\quad [0,\infty)\\
\text{Eternal time:}&\quad (-\infty,\infty)
\end{aligned}}
$$

The theory doesn't have to choose between them philosophically.

It can treat them as different temporal structures.

---

# 13. A possible name

I would call the formal theory:

# **Infinite-State Temporal Theory (ISTT)**

with the larger philosophical framework:

# **FuturLang Temporal Theory**

And the core object:

$$
\boxed{\mathfrak T=(S,\rightarrow)}
$$

could be called a **Temporal State Machine**.

Then your hierarchy becomes:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Finite State Machine}
\subset
\text{Infinite State Machine}
\subset
\text{Temporal State Machine}
\subset
\text{Universal State Space}
}
$$

The really interesting research question is then:

> **Can the mathematical structure of time be derived from the properties of the transition relation rather than postulated as an independent coordinate?**

That is a serious mathematical-philosophical question. It connects naturally to dynamical systems, automata theory, graph theory, formal languages, computation, and the foundations of physics—while keeping the **Futurlang distinction between state, observation, proof, and prediction** at the center.
